🔍 Key Insights & Findings


Underutilized Infrastructure


The network has high stand capacity (54K) but only 18K bikes deployed


Indicates inefficient asset utilization




Low Overall Utilization (34%)


Nearly two-thirds of docking capacity remains unused


Opportunity for better fleet distribution




Service Reliability Issue


669 open stations have zero bikes


Users cannot start trips at these locations




City-level Variation


Utilization varies significantly by contract (city)


Some cities operate efficiently, others are under-used




Banking Stations Opportunity


327 stations support payments


Potential to analyze whether banking improves usage and where to expand it





📈 Business Impact


Identifies rebalancing needs to reduce zero-bike stations


Highlights cities with poor utilization for targeted intervention


Supports capacity right-sizing and redistribution strategies


Improves customer trust and service reliability



✅ Conclusion
The Power BI dashboard reveals that the bike-sharing network has strong infrastructure but low operational efficiency.
Despite large station and stand capacity, low utilization and a high number of zero-bike stations indicate rebalancing and planning gaps.
By addressing these issues—especially in underperforming cities—the system can significantly improve usage, reliability, and user satisfaction.
